---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About St. John of Kronstadt Orthodox Mission | True Orthodox Church, Bayview TX"
  description="St. John of Kronstadt Orthodox Mission — True Orthodox parish in Bayview, TX serving the Rio Grande Valley. Service times, sacraments, canonical standing."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 md:py-28">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">Our Parish</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl text-text-primary md:text-4xl">About St. John of Kronstadt Orthodox Mission</h1>
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl text-text-primary md:text-3xl">What is True Orthodoxy?</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 h-px w-12 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
      <p>
        The Orthodox Church is the one, holy, catholic, and apostolic Church — the faith
        delivered by Christ Himself to His Apostles, and handed down without addition or
        subtraction through the centuries to us. It is not a denomination among
        denominations, nor a tradition among traditions. It is <em>the</em> Church —
        there is no other. The Seven Ecumenical Councils defined and defended that faith
        against every heresy that rose to challenge it, and the Orthodox Church has held
        that faith unbroken from Pentecost to this day. Western churches, on the other
        hand, have reformed, divided, and reinvented themselves to cater to fads,
        politics, and social contagions — wandering ever further from the ancient
        instructions of God Himself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Orthodoxy maintains the fullness of what Christ Himself taught and demonstrated —
        prayer, fasting, charity, worship, and loving service to God and neighbor. From
        His instructions to the Apostles, and through the centuries of conciliar wisdom,
        came the Divine Liturgy, the Holy Mysteries, and the whole of the Holy Tradition
        we hold without compromise today. We do not change with the times. We do not
        negotiate with the world. We are the same Church that baptized the martyrs of
        Rome — and because our faith is unchanged and uncompromising, we are persecuted
        even unto this very day. This is why Orthodoxy alone continues to recognize new
        martyrs and saints.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl text-text-primary md:text-3xl">The Catacomb Church</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 h-px w-12 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
      <p>
        In the early twentieth century, the Bolshevik revolution did not merely overthrow
        a government — it set out to destroy a civilization and the faith at its heart.
        On July 17, 1918, Tsar Nicholas II, the Tsaritsa, and their five children were
        executed in a basement in Yekaterinburg by order of the Soviet regime. With them
        died the last canonically Orthodox Christian monarch — and with him, any
        possibility of convening a future Ecumenical Council. The symphony between Church
        and State that had governed Orthodox Christian civilization since Constantine
        requires a divinely appointed Orthodox emperor to call such a council. That man
        was murdered in 1918, deliberately. The consequences are permanent: there will be
        no Eighth Ecumenical Council. There can be no canonical reunion with Rome, or
        with any body that has departed from the ancient faith. The Bolsheviks knew
        exactly what they were destroying.
      </p>
      <p>
        Having murdered the monarchy, the Soviets turned to the Church herself. They
        closed and desecrated the monasteries, executed the clergy by the thousands, and
        subjected the institutional Church to relentless pressure to submit to state
        control. Holy Patriarch Tikhon — foreseeing exactly this catastrophe — had issued
        Decree No. 362 in 1920, providing for the continuation of canonical Orthodox
        church life even if the central administration were destroyed or corrupted. He
        died in April 1925. His designated successors were arrested one by one. The
        regime searched for a hierarch willing to sign what Patriarch Tikhon would not.
      </p>
      <p>
        They found him. On July 29, 1927, Metropolitan Sergius (Stragorodsky) issued his
        infamous Declaration, effectively placing the Russian Church under Soviet state
        control and pledging her loyalty to the godless regime that had already soaked
        Russian soil with the blood of her faithful. It was published in the official
        Soviet newspaper. The majority of bishops, clergy, and laypeople rejected it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Armed with Patriarch Tikhon's Decree No. 362 and with faith that did not waver,
        they continued to celebrate the Divine Liturgy in secret. They were hunted down
        and executed by the communists. In reverent faith in God, they continued to
        gather wherever they could do so safely — in homes, barns, forests, and
        catacombs — becoming known thereafter as the Catacomb Church, and now as the
        True Orthodox Church. The Soviet state that Metropolitan Sergius legitimized with
        his signature would go on to register itself as the "Moscow Patriarchate" in 1943
        — by order of Stalin, who needed the Church's patriotic influence during the war.
        The body Metropolitan Sergius created was a Soviet institution. It remains the
        Moscow Patriarchate today.
      </p>
      <p>
        The price the True Orthodox paid was almost beyond reckoning. The Holy New
        Martyrs Metropolitan Kirill of Kazan and Metropolitan Joseph of Petrograd —
        founding fathers of the Catacomb Church — both died in Soviet captivity in 1937,
        at the height of Stalin's purges. By 1929 alone, more than twenty bishops had
        been arrested simply for refusing to recognize the Sergianist Declaration.
        Hundreds of thousands of clergy, monastics, and laypeople followed them into the
        labor camps, into exile, and into martyrdom. The Soviet state declared the True
        Orthodox Church extinct. They were wrong.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl text-text-primary md:text-3xl">After the Soviet Era</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 h-px w-12 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
      <p>
        When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, the Catacomb Church emerged from the
        underground — not as a relic of the past, but as a living, worshipping community.
        The Orthodox faith had not compromised. It had not collaborated. It had paid an
        almost unimaginable price to keep the faith whole and unaltered, and emerged with
        that faith intact. The Moscow Patriarchate, born of Soviet bureaucracy and
        Stalin's wartime calculation, continues to this day — now serving the political
        interests of the Russian state as faithfully as it once served the Soviet one.
        The True Orthodox Church continues her witness regardless, in communion with
        sister churches around the world, maintaining the ancient liturgical life of the
        Orthodox faith without innovation or compromise.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl text-text-primary md:text-3xl">A Mission Parish in the Rio Grande Valley</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 h-px w-12 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
      <p>
        St. John of Kronstadt Orthodox Mission carries that inheritance to South Texas. A
        parish of the Autonomous Orthodox Metropolia of North and South America and the
        British Isles, we gather in Bayview, Texas under the pastoral care of Fr.
        Antonios Altermatt to celebrate the Divine Liturgy and Vespers in the unbroken
        tradition of the Orthodox faith.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are a small community, and all the more welcoming for it. Whether you are
        encountering Orthodoxy for the first time, preparing for chrismation, or an
        Orthodox Christian visiting the Rio Grande Valley, you are warmly invited to
        come and pray with us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="mx-auto h-px w-full max-w-3xl border-0 bg-liturgical-current opacity-40" />

